rom trafficking and connect displacement to conflict/economic factors; legacy material covers employment-driven movement and settlement pressures. | S5 P5 SCS third term, Introduction to Human Trafficking; S6 P6 SCS second term, weeks 8–10; S7 pp. 249–251 and 259 | Enrichment | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | No direct P6 migration/IDP/refugee objective was found. UNHCR and displaced-person rights are enrichment and require authoritative sourcing during authoring. |\n| p6-socs-09 | Safety and Security — Staying Safe as a Citizen | S4 public unified SCS P4; S5 public unified SCS P5; S6 public unified SCS P6 | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): personal safety, neighbourhood security, and safe choices | Public schemes cover neighbourhood safety, reporting, adult help, cyber awareness, risk assessment, safe choices, and resilience. | S4 P4 SCS third term, Neighbourhood Security/Accident Response; S5 P5 SCS third term, Neighbourhood Security; S6 P6 SCS third term, Safe and Healthy Choices | Enrichment | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Brief intentionally narrows the content to immediate personal safety and prohibits profiling, named groups, tactics, recruitment signs, and conflict-zone examples. Full Hausa sign-off required. |\n| p6-socs-10 | Technology and Society | S1 NERDC implementation context; S6 public unified P6 SCS and Basic Digital Literacy | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): digital citizenship, communication, and online safety | S1 requires digital competencies and responsible technology use; public P6 material covers digital financial services, online safety, internet/social-media safety, privacy, and cyber risks. | S1 pp. 1–3; S6 P6 SCS second term, week 3 and third term, week 8; S6 P6 Basic Digital Literacy, Internet/Social Media Safety | Combined coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Partly cross-curricular. Cyberbullying is not explicit in the cited SCS section but fits the public digital-safety sequence. |\n| p6-socs-11 | Civic Role Models in Nigeria | S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P6 Civic; S6 public unified SCS P6 | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): national service, role models, and civic leadership | Public P6 civic material covers national honours, selfless service, award recipients, patriotism, and nationalists’ achievements; public SCS asks pupils to become role models and agents of change. | S7 pp. 32–33; S6 P6 SCS third term, weeks 9–10 | Combined coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | The five named Nigerians are Architect-selected, not a public scheme list. Keep biography to one sentence and focus on civic contribution; neutrality review required. |\n| p6-socs-12 | Population Growth and Civic Responsibility | S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P5/P6 Social Studies; S6 public unified P6 demography | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): population, services, resources, and planning | Public sources cover population growth, water/housing demand, resource use, census, density, planning, and resource allocation. | S7 pp. 250–252 and 261; S6 P6 Mathematics second term, Demography & Money I | Combined coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Partly cross-curricular. Any population statistic must carry a reference year and authoritative source; avoid coercive family-size messaging. |\n| p6-socs-13 | Entrepreneurship and Self-Reliance | S6 public unified SCS P6; S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P4/P6 Social Studies; S1 context | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): entrepreneurship, work, and national development | Public P6 SCS covers entrepreneurship, financial goals, youth innovation, nation building, and personal commitment; legacy source covers self-employment skills and loans. | S6 P6 SCS second term, week 2 and third term, week 9; S7 pp. 233 and 259; S1 p. 3 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | Bank of Agriculture and BOI are brief-selected institutions and require current official verification at authoring time. |\n| p6-socs-14 | Peace Education and Conflict Prevention | S7 Lagos State MoE legacy P6 Social Studies; S1 context | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): peace, tolerance, cooperation, and conflict resolution | Public P6 state scheme defines peace education and covers tolerance, cooperation, shared responsibility, communal effort, and national unity. | S7 pp. 263–265, Group Conflict and Peace Education; S1 p. 3 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | UNICEF-framework reference is enrichment. Avoid operational conflict details and current conflict actors. |\n| p6-socs-15 | Revision and Consolidation — Bridge to JSS1 | S6 public unified SCS P6 | P6 | Public-source topic family (not official NERDC strand): consolidation and secondary-school citizenship | Public P6 SCS closes with preparation for secondary-school citizenship, comprehensive review, role modelling, leadership, and life-long learning. | S6 P6 SCS third term, weeks 10–12 | Direct coverage | BEST-EFFORT — NOT VERIFIED AGAINST OFFICIAL NERDC GRADE DOCUMENT | The P3–P6 recap is AJAMIX-specific consolidation. |\n\n## Matrix totals and validation summary\n\n- Total rows: **60**.\n- Grade distribution: **P3 15; P4 15; P5 15; P6 15**.\n- Duplicate module IDs: **0**.\n- Missing brief IDs/titles: **0**.\n- Missing classifications: **0**.\n- Classification distribution: **Direct coverage 19; Combined coverage 21; Enrichment 20**.\n- Missing verification statuses: **0**.\n- `mapping uncertain — no public source found`: **0 rows**.\n- Important limitation: zero “mapping uncertain” flags means each proposed module has at least some cited public support; it does **not** mean any row is verified against the official NERDC grade document.\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-01\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-01.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Classifying Living Things\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Rarraba Abubuwa Masu Rai\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A split scene: left side shows two or three plants (e.g. maize, beans, grass) with visible roots, stem, leaves; right side shows two or three animals of different kinds (a hen, a fish in water, a goat on land) to show classification into plants vs animals, with a small habitat cue for each animal (water, land).\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Tsirrai\", \"Dabbobi\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No people depicted touching or handling the animals; keep the scene a calm outdoor/farm setting, not a market or slaughter context.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-02\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-02.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Parts of the Body and Their Functions\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Sassan Jiki da Ayyukansu\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A friendly, fully-clothed child illustration (front view) with simple labeled pointer lines to eyes, ears, nose, mouth, hands, and legs/feet — each doing its function subtly implied (e.g. eyes looking, hand holding a book).\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Idanu\", \"Kunnuwa\", \"Hanci\", \"Baki\", \"Hannu\", \"Ƙafafu\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Non-graphic, fully clothed child, no internal body parts (heart/skin layers) shown beyond a simple external labeled figure.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-03\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-03.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Growth and Life Cycles\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Girma da Zagayowar Rayuwa\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A three-row life-cycle diagram with looping arrows: row 1 seed to sprout to young plant to flower/seed; row 2 egg to chick to hen/rooster; row 3 baby to child to teenager to adult, shown as simple growth-stage silhouettes.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ƙwai\", \"Kaza\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Human growth row must show only clothed, generic silhouettes at each age stage — no depiction of birth or medical process.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-04\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-04.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Our Environment\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Muhallinmu\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A labeled overview scene of a tidy compound/neighborhood showing a home, a school building, a well with a cover, trees, a path, and a covered waste bin — everything clean and in good repair, illustrating what makes up one's environment.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Kwandon Shara\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Keep the whole scene clean and undamaged (this module is about naming environment components and tidiness habits, not pollution) — no litter, no open well, no smoke.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-05\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-05.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"The Water Cycle\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Zagayowar Ruwa\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A circular water-cycle diagram: sun over a stream/pond with wavy lines rising (evaporation), rising into a cloud, cloud releasing rain onto ground/well/river, arrow looping back to the stream — plus a small inset of a covered water container for clean drinking water.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ƙafewa\", \"Gajimare\", \"Ruwan Sama\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Do not depict anyone drinking directly from an open stream or dirty water source; show a covered container for the clean-water example.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-06\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-06.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"States of Matter\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Yanayin Abu: Abu Mai Tauri, Ruwa, da Iska\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A three-panel comparison: (1) a solid object (a stone or a book) holding its shape, (2) water being poured from a jug into a cup, taking the cup's shape, (3) a light wisp of steam rising from a covered cup of warm water, showing the gas state, with a soft warning glow/steam-only depiction (no visible flame).\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Abu Mai Tauri\", \"Ruwa\", \"Iska\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No open flame or fire; do not show a child's hand near the warm water or steam — the steam panel should be a small, distant wisp only, consistent with the module using hot water only as an adult-supervised example.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-07\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-07.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Measuring Length and Mass\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Auna Tsawo da Nauyi\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Two side-by-side panels: a ruler laid against a pencil showing a centimeter scale, and a simple balance/weighing scale with a small sack of grain on one side, both starting from a visible zero mark.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Santimita\", \"Mita\", \"Giram\", \"Kilogiram\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Show a child using only lightweight, simple tools (ruler, small scale) — no heavy or industrial weighing equipment, consistent with the module's advice to ask an adult for heavy tools.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-08\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-08.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Measuring Time\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Auna Lokaci\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A large analog clock face with the small hour hand and large minute hand clearly distinguished by size/color, plus a small side panel showing a child watering a plant with a start-clock and end-clock pair to illustrate elapsed time.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Agogo\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No safety hazard content needed; keep the scene calm and ordinary (a clock and a watering task).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-09\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-09.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Soil and Its Uses\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Ƙasa da Amfaninta\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A cross-section of ground showing plant roots reaching into soil layered with tiny rock bits and decomposed leaves, beside two small soil-sample panels labeled sandy soil and clay soil, and a small inset showing soil used to make bricks and a clay pot.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Ƙasa Mai Yashi\", \"Ƙasa Mai Laka\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Show the child observing soil only, not tasting it; include a small hand-washing cue since the module tells children to wash hands after touching soil.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-10\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-10.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Air in Motion\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Iska Mai Motsi\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Two panels: (1) a calm scene showing wind's effect — clothes flapping on a line and a toy pinwheel spinning, with a light cloth/leaf shown tilting to indicate wind direction; (2) a safety panel showing a child and an adult walking together toward a sturdy shelter away from a tree and a weak wall during a dust storm, covering nose and mouth.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No open flame anywhere in the image (the module warns against using fire to check wind direction); the storm panel must show the child accompanied by an adult, at a safe distance from trees and weak walls, not alone.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-11\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-11.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Traditional and Modern Technology\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Kayan Aikin Gargajiya da na Zamani\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A side-by-side comparison: left side shows traditional tools at rest (a hand hoe, a mortar and pestle, a donkey cart), right side shows modern tools at rest (a tractor, a grinding mill, a car), all shown stationary and unattended by children.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Fartanya\", \"Turmi da Taɓarya\", \"Keken Jaki\", \"Babbar Na'urar Noma\", \"Injin Niƙa\", \"Mota\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No child touching, operating, or standing close to any tool or machine; no running engine, no exposed wire or socket, no child near the grinding mill — tools shown idle only, per the module's caution.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-12\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-12.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Light and Mirrors\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Haske da Madubi\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"Two panels: (1) a ray from a safe classroom lamp bouncing from a fixed mirror toward an eye icon, with arrows showing the correct path; (2) the same upright object casting a long morning shadow and a short midday shadow, with the sun positions shown but no person looking toward the sun.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Madubi\", \"Inuwa\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Do not depict anyone looking directly at the sun or angling a mirror's reflection toward another person's or animal's eyes; show the mirror handled gently, not near a fall risk of breaking.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-13\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-13.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"First Aid and Safety\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Taimakon Farko da Tsaro\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A calm three-panel sequence: (1) a child checking the area is safe and calling an adult (raised hand gesture, speech bubble toward an adult figure); (2) an adult gently pressing a clean cloth on a child's arm (no wound visible, just the cloth and a caring expression); (3) an adult holding a child's hand under a running tap of cool water with a small clock icon showing time passing, for a burn.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No visible blood, open wound, burn mark, or injury detail of any kind — depict only the calm, competent care actions (checking safety, calling an adult, clean cloth, cool running water); do not show oil, toothpaste, or ice being applied; do not depict broken glass, a fallen wire, floodwater, or smoke up close — those hazards should only be implied by the child pointing/stepping away.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-14\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-14.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Animal Habitats and Shelters\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Mazauni da Mafakar Dabbobi\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A set of small labeled panels: a fish with visible fins swimming in water, a duck with webbed feet on a pond, a bird's nest built in a tree holding eggs, a rabbit at the entrance of a burrow, and a farm scene with a ventilated chicken coop and a shaded goat/sheep shelter with clean water nearby.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Sheƙa\", \"Rami\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Do not depict a child reaching into, disturbing, or breaking a wild animal's nest or burrow — observation only, from a distance, per the module's instruction.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-15\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-15.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Balanced Meals\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Abinci Mai Gina Jiki\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A plate divided into three labeled sections with everyday Nigerian foods: energy foods (tuwo, shinkafa, gero, dankali), body-building foods (wake, kifi, ƙwai, nama), and protective foods (alayyahu, zogale, and a fruit like lemu or ayaba), with a cup of clean water beside the plate.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"Depict only ordinary, affordable, locally available foods already named in the module text — no sugary sweets/alewa shown as part of the balanced plate, and no depiction of a child's body size.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"p3-bsci-16\",\n    \"imagePath\": \"images/p3-bsci-16.png\",\n    \"titleEn\": \"Plant Parts and Functions (Deeper Study)\",\n    \"titleHa\": \"Sassan Tsiro da Ayyukansu: Nazari Mai Zurfi\",\n    \"type\": \"diagram\",\n    \"depictEn\": \"A labeled cross-section of a whole plant showing the root system with fine rootlets in soil, small internal transport lines running up through the stem to the leaves (with arrows showing water/nutrients moving up), a leaf catching sunlight, and a flower transitioning into a fruit (e.g. tumatir or kabewa) containing visible seeds.\",\n    \"labelsHa\": [\"Tushe\", \"Kara\", \"Ganye\", \"Fure\"],\n    \"safetyNote\": \"No depiction of a farmer or child breaking the stem or tearing leaves — the diagram should show only the intact healthy plant and its internal pathways, not the damage scenario described in the text.\"\n  }\n]\nperl: warning: Setting locale failed.\nperl: warning: Please check that your locale settings:\n\tLC_ALL = \"C.UTF-8\",\n\tLC_CTYPE = \"C.UTF-8\",\n\tLANG = \"C.UTF-8\"\n    are supported and installed on your system.\nperl: warning: Falling back to the standard locale (\"C\").\npanic: locale.c: 4486: Could not change LC_CTYPE locale to C.UTF-8, errno=9\n","exit_code":9,"status":"failed"}}
